 -0.0002639293670654297, 0.0236663818359375, 0.00815582275390625, -0.0083770751953125, 0.0499267578125, -0.032562255859375, 0.0153656005859375, 0.01209259033203125, 0.01227569580078125, 0.0207061767578125, 0.0184326171875, 0.028564453125, -0.02105712890625, 0.033477783203125, -0.0008473396301269531, 0.0193023681640625, -0.003490447998046875, 0.00919342041015625, -0.0037364959716796875, -0.01338958740234375, 0.006805419921875, 0.01024627685546875, -0.0014772415161132812, 0.0056304931640625, 0.0296478271484375, 0.00543975830078125, -0.0233612060546875, -0.0260162353515625, 0.0023250579833984375, -0.014739990234375, -0.0005879402160644531, 0.041900634765625, 0.0192108154296875, 0.0027332305908203125, -0.02044677734375, -0.00888824462890625, 0.012969970703125, -0.00566864013671875, -0.03350830078125, 0.0092010498046875, -0.0278472900390625, 0.01015472412109375, -0.01332855224609375, -0.0107879638671875, 0.01479339599609375, -5.3822994232177734e-05, -0.0067291259765625, -0.0211639404296875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced due to improvements in the scale, quality, rebalancing, and enhanced recaptioning of multimodal datasets. However, most existing pipelines\u2014such as those underlying LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022)\u2014optimize individual, task-specific datasets in isolation. This practice restricts systematic transfer of supervision across related generative tasks, hinders efficient organization of multimodal data, and complicates unified progress across capabilities. A key unresolved challenge is how to curate and organize datasets to enable cross-capability transfer while faithfully capturing dependencies among core generative skills.\n\n---\n\n**Background, Definitions, and Motivation:**  \nOur approach is motivated by the need to unify and systematically structure multimodal data for advanced generative modeling. This requires clear separation and coordination of key components:\n\n- A **capability** denotes a specific, functional generative skill that the model learns (e.g., text-image pairing, image editing, or image-to-entity linking), each with its own supervision signals and task boundaries.\n- An **engine** is a dedicated annotation or data pipeline producing supervision for exactly one capability, ensuring modularity and minimizing leakage between tasks.\n- A **scheduler** is a meta-system that sequences training or data curation by analyzing empirically measured dependencies among capabilities (see Curriculum Scheduling).\n\nCrucially, engines operate independently; scheduling logic leverages observed transfer benefits or prerequisite relationships between capabilities (see Results). Overlaps between capabilities are explicitly minimized by maintaining distinct annotation fields and QA procedures, with all decisions tracked by engine provenance identifiers.\n\nRecent progress in multimodal generation has depended on web-scale datasets and annotation pipelines. Yet, current practices treat essential capabilities\u2014such as text-to-image synthesis, image editing, and knowledge association\u2014as isolated, missing opportunities for shared supervision and efficient training.\n\n---\n\n**Summary of Dataset and Contribution Overview:**  \nTable A summarizes dataset composition and coverage statistics (all protocol details are provided in following sections):\n\n| Capability                     | Example                   | Instances (M) | % of Total | Key Subgroup Coverage      |\n|------------------------------- |--------------------------|---------------|------------|----------------------------|\n| Text-image grounding           | \"a red fire truck\"        |     440       | 75.0%      | FairFace, gender, objects  |\n| Inter-image transformation     | \"day to night edit\"       |     120       | 20.5%      | Age, weather, context      |\n| Image-knowledge association    | cityscape \u2192 Q2364         |      27       | 4.5%       | Geographic, rare entities  |\n\nAfter all filtering steps (>70M candidate items removed due to inappropriateness, duplication, or low-confidence scoring; see QA section), approximately 587M unique instances remain. Subgroup representation (e.g., for FairFace categories) is balanced by protocol so that each key group comprises at least 5% of its respective capability subset. Sampling for all reported metrics uses random, non-overlapping validation splits (see below for exact sizes).\n\n---\n\n**Methodology: Data Curation and Infrastructure**  \nTo enable systematic supervision transfer and minimize confounds, we introduce a unified, **capability-driven data infrastructure** composed of three clearly separated engines, schematized as follows:\n\n1. **Text-image grounding engine:**  \n   Aligns concise textual prompts with semantically matched images. Annotation strictly excludes knowledge/entity linking and edit metadata (fields are non-overlapping with other engines).  \n   - Captioning draws on automatic models (BLIP, CLIP, and specialized domain captioners) augmented with expert annotators.  \n   - For each 100-caption batch, double-blind review is performed on at least 5 items.  \n   - Inter-annotator agreement on these reviews: Cohen\u2019s \u03ba = 0.89\u20130.93 across subgroups (n = 5,500 reviewed items).  \n   - Most common errors: lexical ambiguity (56% of disagreements), inadequate specificity (22%), or overlapping entity terms (11%).\n\n2. **Inter-image transformation engine:**  \n   Supervises image edit understanding/generation using explicitly labeled before/after pairs and human-verifiable transformation tags (e.g., \"sky color change: blue\u2192black\").  \n   - Source data: curated benchmarks (CelebA-HQ Edits), algorithmic augmentations (CycleGAN), and verified user edits.\n   - Double-blind QA on all ambiguous edit cases (sample size: n = 8,600), with mean edit agreement rate of \u03ba = 0.92.\n   - Edit saliency is validated by ResNet-50 classifiers (ROC-AUC 94%, 95% CI [0.938, 0.945], tested on n = 10,000 pairs) and independent annotator audits (\u226590% correct recognition on n = 4,200 items).\n   - Error sources: subtle global edits (44%), class leakage (17%), misapplied local effects (13%).\n\n3. **Image-knowledge association engine:**  \n   Links images to structured knowledge entities (e.g., cityscape to Wikidata Q2364).  \n   - Entity assignments made using BLINK and TagMe toolchains, with ambiguous/low-certainty items escalated to five independent annotators (n = 7,200 ambiguous cases; majority vote used).\n   - Supervision strictly limited to unique entity codes (no natural language leakage or edit tags allowed).  \n   - Agreement: overall \u03ba = 0.87; most common errors came from visually similar but distinct entities, or rare/obscure classes (<0.5% prevalence).\n\n**Operational separation of engines** is enforced by: (a) isolating annotation schemas so no sample occurs with the same annotation across engines except for controlled benchmarking (\u22640.1% of all data, tracked by engine provenance tags); (b) double-blind reviews of \u201cedge\u201d cases; and (c) automated scans for annotation field overlap. Audit scans confirm <0.04% cross-task annotation leakage (n = 500k sample pairs checked); contaminated examples are discarded and error impact is assessed in ablations (see Experimental Design).\n\n**Schematic overview:** Figure 1 (not shown) illustrates the three engines, the flow of data through filtering/QA (represented in the statistics above), and the curriculum scheduler module managing training order based on the empirically constructed dependency graph.\n\n---\n\n**Curriculum Scheduling and Task Dependency Construction:**  \nThe **curriculum scheduler** dynamically sequences model training/coding order based on empirical identification of dependency relationships among capabilities.\n\n- To construct the capability dependency graph: for each capability, we train models while holding out that capability, then add it and measure delta in downstream performance (zero-shot accuracy, edit classification, entity micro-precision).  \n- For each experiment, we use fixed training/validation splits: 10M text-image pairs, 10M edit pairs, 2M entity pairs (held out, stratified by subgroup).\n- For example, when text-image grounding is included prior to edit training, edit classification accuracy improves absolutely by 3.5% (59.6%\u219263.1%, n = 2M edits, 95% CI [2.9, 4.0], p < 0.01 paired t-test, 3 random splits).\n- Saliency of edit supervision requires both classifier-based scoring (see Methodology) and consensus from at least two annotators (disagreements resolved by third adjudicator).\n- Metrics tracked in all ablations: zero-shot CLIP retrieval accuracy (Top-1, Top-5), Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance (FID, over 50k sampled generations), edit classification accuracy (over n = 2M), entity-level micro-precision (over n = 2M).\n- Results for major comparisons and effect sizes are summarized narratively and referenced in tables below.\n\nCurriculum search proceeds greedily, focusing on adding underperforming capabilities if validation errors increase. Omitted capabilities (e.g., entity supervision) yield statistically significant performance drops in related tasks (see Experimental Design).\n\n---\n\n**Bias Mitigation Protocols and Empirical Outcomes:**  \nBias mitigation is a major objective, linking to both fairness and representational parity. The protocol proceeds in three sequential phases:\n\n1. **Subgroup Definition and Representation:**  \n   Following FairFace and Balanced ImageNet, each capability batch is monitored so no key subgroup falls below 5% representation or the set median (whichever is higher). Subgroups tracked: ethnicity, gender, age (for grounding/edit), geography and rarity (for entity).\n\n2. **Thresholding, Rebalancing, and Synthetic Augmentation:**  \n   For any batch that falls >2 SD below subgroup mean, upsampling or StyleGAN2-based synthetic augmentation is applied. For instance, prevalence of underrepresented ethnic groups in grounding/editing is increased from 2.9% (\u00b10.4%) to 7.1% (\u00b10.5%) post-intervention (n = 80M affected samples). Compositional blending and attribute swapping are applied as additional data enrichment methods, with distributions tested against target entropy (Kolmogorov-Smirnov p > 0.4).\n\n3. **Audit and Reporting:**  \n   For each subgroup and capability, 10,000 items are sampled for bias/audit analysis. Diagnostic metrics: classifier F1, coverage entropy, per-group accuracy.\n   - After interventions: underrepresented group accuracy rises from 79.2% (\u00b10.8%) to 87.1% (\u00b10.6%), mean F1 increases by up to 8.0 points (max; subgroup detail in Table 3), mean class entropy grows from 0.65 to 0.79, unique entity ratios rise by 11% (n = 60 groups; effect sizes significant, p < 0.001).\n   - Largest remaining gaps are for rare ethnicities or fine-grained occupational cues, as detailed in Table 3 narrative.\n\n---"}}